203
00:19:40,028 --> 00:19:47,139
So, you say, "Okay, everyone talks about agents, but what do you actually need uh, for agents?"

204
00:19:47,189 --> 00:19:53,648
And I think the most important thing to realize is that agents actually are not one thing.

205
00:19:53,698 --> 00:19:57,356
You know, agents, like people, there are lots of different types of agents.

206
00:19:57,406 --> 00:20:01,270
You need the right agents for the right um, application.

207
00:20:01,320 --> 00:20:04,159
And for that, you actually need the right compute.

208
00:20:04,209 --> 00:20:09,585
So, when you're talking about personal agents, you probably want the densest agents possible.

209
00:20:09,635 --> 00:20:14,618
These are simpler tasks, and you want them to be uh, you know, very efficient.

210
00:20:14,668 --> 00:20:19,589
You want them to be maximum number of agents per watt, so you are uh, saving your power correctly.

211
00:20:19,639 --> 00:20:21,913
Um, you know, that's one class of agents.

212
00:20:21,963 --> 00:20:28,034
Uh, you talk about enterprise agents, where you're doing a lot of different types of activities, and so you want them to be pretty balanced.

213
00:20:28,084 --> 00:20:33,710
Or you're talking about some of the hardest compute problems out there, these technical compute agents.

214
00:20:33,760 --> 00:20:38,643
You're going You're going to want them to be bleeding-edge performance, kind of the the best of the best.

215
00:20:38,693 --> 00:20:42,490
And so, each of these agents needs a different type of compute.

216
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:48,064
And what we believe is each of these agents also need the right type of rack.

217
00:20:48,114 --> 00:20:57,630
And that means you're going to see a certain type of rack in hyperscale environments, where you might have disaggregated CPU and GPU racks.

218
00:20:57,680 --> 00:21:03,310
Whereas in enterprise, you might want to see more all-in-one racks uh, going forward.

